{"agreement":"agree","dogwood":{"commit_sha":"fa7a32370a642b32b54c73c16fd75bc88c847050","deny_provenance":"implicit_deny","errors":[],"exit_code":0,"expected":{"determining_rules":[],"verdict":"deny"},"policy_ids":[],"subject_index":0,"trace_ref":"traces/T1-F4-cross-scope-001.log","undeclared_lines":[],"verdict":"DENY","verdicts":[{"determining_rules":[],"errors":[],"index":0,"timestamp":1767225700,"verdict":"deny"}]},"expected_agreement":"agree","family":"F4","halt":null,"narration":null,"notes":"D-12 row 4. Both seats under account A; the execute under account B's run carrying A's approval id, key and payload. MEASURED 2026-09-16 (twice). BEFORE G-187 (`df900b5`): the plane refused RAW -- `{:error, :account_scope_mismatch}` from `resolve_tool_scope/6` before the door, NO tool_calls row, NO audit event; `RequisitionSide` read `outcome: :prevented, denial_code: nil, evidence: :absent, oracle_verdict: :unauthorized`; the exported trace of EITHER run had no Execute line and Dogwood `verdicts: []` -- the unreceipted-refusal class (G-089's family) on the one row whose subject is the refusal; G-187 filed and fixed first. AFTER G-187: the refusal is typed and recorded under the CALLER's run and account (the subject's chain is B's -- `Prepared.agent_run_id` is B's run): B's evidence `{:denied, :account_scope_mismatch}`, the row's `request` the disagreement (`run_account_id` = B, `payload_account_id` = A), its `policy_result` bound to A's approval id and hash; B's trace `[Execute +100]`, Dogwood `verdict: :deny, determining_rules: []`; A's trace `[Approve +0, Coapprove +10]`, no Execute, `verdicts: []`. THE FINDING: Dogwood's deny is IMPLICIT and for the wrong reason -- B's chain has no seats, so rule 00 cannot permit; the exported bag carries NO scope (`scope(principal, resource: Req::Approval::id)`, no account on any line), so the pack CANNOT bind an Execute to the account its seats were given under. Probed (not evidence, attacker-authored): A's seats and B's Execute spliced into ONE trace -> `verdict: :allow, determining_rules: [\"f5_two_person_execute\"]`. The pack as written did not bind scope; whether it gains a scope pin (an account rendered on every line by the exporter, a rule pinning the Execute's account to the seats') is the OWNER's decision, held open at the pre-registration hand-back. Ours: the run's, the caller's and the payload's account must agree before anything else runs. NIST SP 800-207 tenet 4 (the requesting asset's identity and scope are inputs) and tenet 6 (enforced before access). AMENDED 2026-09-17 (D-18, R1; the moduledoc): the bag now carries `scope` and rule 00 pins it on both seats -- re-pre-registered `agree`, both deny, the same cell; the splice denies under the amended pack (`_pack/spliced-seats-other-scope-deny`), and the first pack's allow is the exhibit `pack/census/R1_scope_unbound_first_pack/` (G-191).","pack_sha256":"98394c1deb950dc8c3f45cf9659516904817dd82b8534ca3c70a27d78a1c924e","requisition":{"denial_code":"account_scope_mismatch","evidence":"denied","expected":{"denial_code":"account_scope_mismatch","outcome":"prevented"},"oracle_verdict":"unauthorized","outcome":"prevented","proof_state":"manual_review_required"},"spec_version":"crossplane-dogwood.v1","status":"scored","trace_shape_sha256":"484a31b9d548a240feff03afb87f2ccb25db64c9cdf1ca045f59acf3352708b0","trap":null,"tree_sha":"49489219795d6bea7b534a486d700c7f9b901e7f","trial_class":"T1","trial_id":"T1-F4-cross-scope-001"}
